{
  "dom_id": "question-166815",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "To complete his work, Mike has $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ liters of paint. Of this amount, he uses $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ liters. How much paint did Mike have left over when he finished? Express your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Mike had$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$liters of paint left.",
  "worked_solution": "After finishing, Mike had $(4\\,\\frac{4}{5}-3\\,\\frac{3}{10})$ liters of paint left.\n\nWriting $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 5+4}{5}=\\frac{24}{5}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10+3}{10}=\\frac{33}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}-\\frac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{24}{5}$ over a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}=\\frac{24\\times 2}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{48}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{10}-\\frac{33}{10}=\\frac{15}{10}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike had $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of paint left.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
